  "description": "Code that prints, writes or sends every environment variable, name and value, instead of one named variable: a Python loop over os.environ.items() whose output call holds only the key and value names, string literals and format fields over them (value included), print(dict(os.environ)) or json.dumps(dict(os.environ)), the mapping as an HTTP body, process.env as the whole argument of a console or write call, a loop over Object.entries(process.env) or a for-in over process.env that logs the value as it is, or printenv/export -p redirected to a file or piped to curl, wget, ncat, netcat, socat, base64 and similar. Environment variables hold API keys and tokens, so this is the shape of a program that quietly hands them to whatever reads its output. One named variable (os.environ['HOME'], process.env.NODE_ENV, printenv HOME), a names-only, count-only or length-only list, a loop that prints only the key, a fixed message, or a value that is compared, sliced, transformed, wrapped in another call or used in a conditional, a filtered loop, an assertion, a JSON replacer list, env | grep X, a redirect to /dev/null, line comments, and passing the environment to a child process are not matched. A bare env, env piped to nc, and a few other shapes listed in the rule's tests are not detected. Statement-shaped text inside a docstring, block comment, README code fence or shell here-document can match. Added after a scanner review of a skill whose script ended in an unconditional print loop over os.environ.items().",
  "keywords": [],
